h1 [fact] September's payroll estimate is 29,000, short of the surveys, and the bureau says that change is not statistically significant
p [fact] The Bureau of Labor Statistics said that gain and the 4.2 percent unemployment rate both changed little. July and August together are 60,000 lower than previously reported. 1
h2 [fact] What happened
p [narrative] On Friday, October 2, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern, the Bureau of Labor Statistics published the Employment Situation for September. 3 Payroll employment rose by 29,000 and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent. The bureau said both changed little. 1
p [narrative] Private payrolls rose by 46,000. 1 Government payrolls fell by 17,000. 1
p [narrative] The July change was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. 1 The August change was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. 1 July and August together are 60,000 lower than previously reported. 1
p [narrative] The August release had reported that payroll employment increased by 162,000 and that the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent. 4
p [narrative] The payroll figure and the unemployment rate come from two surveys. “The household survey measures labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry.” 1 September's payroll change is the establishment figure, and the unemployment rate is the household figure.
p [narrative] Average hourly earnings increased by 3 percent over the past 12 months. 1 On September 16 the Federal Reserve's committee decided to “raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent.” 5 The Personal Income and Outlays release of Wednesday, September 30, 2026, reports the PCE price index. 6 The PCE price index for August increased 3.4 percent from the same month a year earlier. 6 PCE is the release's abbreviation for personal consumption expenditures. 6
p [] On Friday, October 2, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern, the Bureau of Labor Statistics published the Employment Situation for September. Payroll employment rose by 29,000 and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent. The bureau said both changed little. The unemployment rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March, and 7.1 million people were unemployed. 1 3
p [] July was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. August was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. The August release had reported a gain of 162,000. The two months together are 60,000 lower than previously reported. 1 4
summary [] July and August, as the September release states them
p [] Employment in every major industry changed little. Health care added 17,000, slower than its average monthly gain of 33,000 over the prior 12 months. Ambulatory care added 13,000 and hospitals 12,000. Nursing and residential care lost 9,000. Construction added 11,000. Manufacturing added 9,000 and is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025. Financial activities lost 7,000 and are down 129,000 since a peak in May 2025, most of that in insurance carriers and related activities. 1
p [] The one-month diffusion index for 250 private industries was 49.0 in September after 57.6 in August. The release counts it as the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment, and says 50 percent is an equal balance between industries adding and cutting jobs. 1 Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000. Information fell by 10,000. Temporary help services fell by 10.9 thousand. 1
p [] Average hourly earnings edged up 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the year they are up 3.0 percent. The average workweek stayed at 34.4 hours. A change of about 122,000 jobs is what the bureau calls statistically significant in the employer survey, so the 29,000 gain does not clear that bar. The household survey's bar is about 650,000. 1
p [] The Associated Press called that yearly wage gain “the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021”. 22
p [] CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. CNBC's 34.6 hours is not the release's figure. The release says the workweek remained at 34.4 hours. 17 1
p [] The unemployment rate for Black workers rose to 7.0 percent. The bureau said that rate increased, and that the rates for adult men (3.9 percent), adult women (3.6 percent), teenagers (14.5 percent), and White (3.6), Asian (2.9), and Hispanic (4.7) workers showed little change. The labor force participation rate was 61.8 percent and the employment-population ratio was 59.2 percent. The labor force rose by 485,000 and household employment by 406,000. 1
p [] Job losers and people who completed temporary jobs fell by 45,000. Job leavers fell by 173,000. Reentrants rose by 152,000 and new entrants rose by 116,000. The summary table counts those groups in thousands. 1
p [] The long-term unemployed, out of work 27 weeks or more, were essentially unchanged at 1.9 million and were 27.1 percent of unemployed people. People employed part time for economic reasons were little changed at 4.5 million. They would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs. 1 People marginally attached to the labor force fell by 236,000 to 1.5 million. Discouraged workers were little changed at 414,000. 1
p [] The broader underemployment rate, U-6, was 7.6 percent in September, after 7.7 percent in August. 1 11
p [] The household survey counted 8,986,000 people working more than one job in September, 5.5 percent of everyone employed, against 8,805,000 in August. The table counts those people in thousands. 2
p [] Prime-age participation, the share of people age 25 to 54 who are working or looking for work, was 83.7 percent in September, up from 83.4 percent in August. 12
p [] On the hourly-earnings series, September's $37.81 is 3.02 percent above September 2025. The release rounds that change to 3.0 percent. 13 1
h2 [fact] The catch
p [narrative] USA Today headlined the release “September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up”. 24 In the establishment survey, an over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant, and the household survey's threshold is about 650,000. 1 The bureau's word for the 29,000 change was that it changed little. 1
p [narrative] The household count of people employed rose by 406,000 from August. 1 The rise in the number of people employed is under the household survey's bar of about 650,000. 1 The labor force rose by 485,000. 1 The number of people unemployed rose by 78,000. 1 The unemployment rate is the number unemployed as a percent of the labor force. 1
p [narrative] A St. Louis Fed note dated Oct. 2, 2026, 16 says more precise data show the unemployment rate “increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%.” 16
p [narrative] The unemployment rate for people who are Black increased, to 7 percent. 1 On the bureau's row for Black or African American workers, July was 6.3 percent and August was 6 percent. 1 A year earlier the rate was 7.6 percent, and the change from August was 1 point. 1
p [narrative] ADP's own private-sector count “increased by 90,000 jobs in September”. 15 The bureau's private payroll line rose by 46,000. 1
p [narrative] “A Labor Department measure of gross hiring – before subtracting those who quit or lose their jobs – has been stuck in a rut for more than two years.” 22 That hiring count, before quits and job losses are subtracted, is not the payroll change of 29,000.
h2 [fact] Where this sits
figcaption [] Jobs gained and lost each month October 2025 to September 2026 · Seasonally adjusted
p [] September: +29,000 jobs . Inspect the source data
p [] BLS via FRED, as published October 3, 2026; later releases may revise them.
summary [] Read the values and calculation
p [] Each bar subtracts the previous month's total jobs from that month's total. FRED reports totals in thousands.
p [narrative] September's gain of 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months. 1 The August release had reported a gain of 162,000, with unemployment unchanged at 4.1 percent. 4 The unemployment rate has remained in a narrow range of 4.1 percent to 4.3 percent since March. 1
p [narrative] The Associated Press put average gains at 68,000 jobs a month so far this year. 22 It put 2025 at 9,700 jobs a month. 22
p [narrative] A Reuters survey of economists had nonfarm payrolls likely up by 90,000 last month. 19 Estimates in that survey ran from 35,000 to 180,000. 19 September's gain of 29,000 is below the low of 35,000. 1 19
p [] September's 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months, the figure the release states. Its three-month average line puts July through September at 51,000. 1
p [] On the current payroll series, the twelve monthly changes through August average 45,250 jobs. 4 of the last twelve months were declines. The nine months of 2026 average 68,000 jobs. September's level is 159,044,000 and August's revised level is 159,015,000. The difference is 29,000, the same change the release reports. 9
summary [] How those averages were counted
p [] Each month is that month's payroll total minus the month before, from the series saved October 3. The twelve months before September, September 2025 through August 2026, sum to 543 thousand and divide by 12 to 45,250. The release states that average as 45,000. The last twelve months include four declines: October 2025, December 2025, February 2026, and July 2026. The 2026 average is January through September, 612 thousand divided by 9.
p [] CNBC reported that economists surveyed by Dow Jones had looked for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1 percent. Reuters, writing before the release, said its survey of economists expected 90,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate for a third month. CBS reported a FactSet forecast of 90,000. The Associated Press said economists had expected about 90,000. 17 19 21 22
h2 [fact] What we do not know yet
p [narrative] In the establishment survey, estimates for the most recent two months are labeled preliminary because they are based on incomplete returns. 1 Hires changed little at 5.2 million in August. 7 The Job Openings and Labor Turnover release for September is scheduled for Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. Eastern. 7 On the Fed's 2026 meeting calendar, October 27-28 follows the September 15-16 meeting. 8 The Employment Situation for October is scheduled for Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern. 1
p [narrative] The September employment release gives no reason for the size of the 29,000 payroll change. The latest Federal Reserve policy decision among the records of this release is the September 16 statement, which does not decide the October 27-28 meeting.
h2 [fact] Chronology
p [] The Employment Situation for October is scheduled for Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern. The bureau's release calendar lists the same morning. 1 3
p [] The Associated Press set the report “a month before voters go to the polls in pivotal midterm elections”. 22
