VERDICT: ENTAILED## What You're Missing

The [built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html) reports the headline numbers and revisions correctly, but its default **The story** mode hides the detailed sourced blocks. That leaves readers without the sampling limit on the 29,000 estimate, the household survey’s countervailing gains, and the sharp rise in Black unemployment. It also calls a change in **net payroll jobs** a slowdown in **hiring**, which is a different measure. The facts view contains a separate 1,000-fold unit error.

The specified reader-model file does not exist. I graded passage groups against the seven reader questions in your instructions; the consequential omissions change *what happened*, *the easiest wrong reading*, *why it matters*, or *what remains unresolved*.

## Is There More to the Story?

**Materially more to the story.** The weak payroll estimate and downward revisions are real. The broader record also shows rising household employment and participation, a much smaller change in the unrounded unemployment rate, a different private payroll estimate from ADP, and a recent gross-hiring measure that does not establish a September hiring decline. These records qualify the page’s implied reading; they do not erase the weak BLS payroll result.

## Completeness Verdict

**Materially misleading by omission in the default story view.** The cited figures alone do not settle whether the labor market deteriorated in September. The erroneous multiple-jobholder count also needs correction in the facts view.

## Critical and Major Findings

1. **Major, grade B: the payroll estimate needs its uncertainty beside the headline.** Page bytes: “payroll employment rose by 29,000.” The [pinned BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:454) gives “about 122,000” as the threshold for a statistically significant monthly establishment-survey change; its technical note labels the newest estimates preliminary. The correct account is a **+29,000 point estimate**, below that threshold, alongside a meaningful 60,000 downward revision to July and August. Put that distinction in the story view so readers do not treat the sign of one month’s estimate as settled.

2. **Major, grade B: the rounded unemployment rise has countervailing evidence.** Page bytes: “unemployment ticks up.” In the [BLS household summary](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:328), the exact rows show labor force change “485,” employed change “406,” and unemployed change “78,” all in thousands. [St. Louis Fed calculations](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september) put the unrounded rate at “4.1413%” in August and “4.1754%” in September. The correct account is a small increase in unemployment **while employment and participation also rose**. This changes the easiest wrong reading of the headline. Those household figures appear in Just the facts, but the default story hides them.

3. **Major, grade B: the national rate conceals a substantial group difference.** Page bytes: “unemployment rate was 4.2 percent.” The [BLS summary row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:348) reads “Black or African American | 7.6 | 6.3 | 6.0 | 7.0 | 1.0.” The correct account is that Black unemployment rose **one percentage point from August to September**, even as the national rounded rate moved one tenth. The story view should carry that distributional change. The same row shows 7.0 percent remained below September 2025’s 7.6 percent, so this is a monthly finding, not a claim of yearlong deterioration.

4. **Major, grade B: “hiring” is the wrong measured quantity.** Page bytes: “shows slowdown in hiring.” The September BLS release measures the **net change in payroll employment**. The latest available [BLS JOLTS record](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_09292026.pdf) says August “Hires changed little at 5.2 million” and schedules September gross-hiring data for November 3. The correct account is that **net payroll growth slowed from August’s revised gain**; September gross hiring had not yet been reported. Use “payroll growth” in the headline, and distinguish hiring and separations when discussing the labor market’s movement.

5. **Major, grade B: the page gives no independent countercheck or adequate baseline for its slowdown reading.** Page bytes: “+29,000” and “Where the month sits.” The [BLS release’s private-sector row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:393) ends “| 46 |,” meaning **+46,000 private payroll jobs**; government’s row ends “| -17 |.” [ADP’s separate payroll record](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September) says “Private employers added 90,000 jobs in September.” These are different surveys, not directly interchangeable results. The [pinned PAYEMS series](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/PAYEMS-2026-10-03.csv) also yields about **9,700 jobs per month in 2025** against **68,000 in January through September 2026**. The correct account is a weak September within a year stronger than 2025, with a separate measure showing stronger September private growth. The threshold for payroll growth that holds unemployment steady is itself disputed: [Dallas Fed’s estimate](https://www.dallasfed.org/research/economics/2026/0331) approached zero in late 2025, but it is not a verified September 2026 threshold.

6. **Major, grade B: the consequence and next decision are outside the chronology.** Page bytes: “Next Employment Situation, for October.” The [Federal Reserve’s September 16 statement](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm) says it raised its policy-rate range by “1/4 percentage point”; its [calendar](https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm) places the next meeting on October 27–28. The [BEA’s August record](https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-august-2026) gives 3.4 percent annual PCE inflation. The correct account is that this jobs release arrives between a rate hike and the next policy decision, while elevated inflation complicates the interpretation. The page should explain that stake without predicting the Fed’s decision.

7. **Major: the multiple-jobholder figure is off by a factor of 1,000.** Page bytes: “counted 8,986 people working more than one job.” The [pinned Table A-9](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09.txt:219) states “[Numbers in thousands],” and its [multiple-jobholder row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09.txt:274) ends “| 8,805 | 8,986 |.” The correct account is **8.986 million people in September**, up **181,000** from August. The page’s next sentence acknowledges the table’s units but does not fix its stated count.

## Before, After, and Around the Story

**Before:** August’s initially reported +162,000 was revised to +133,000, and July to −10,000, as the [September BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:310) states. The August [JOLTS release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_09292026.pdf) supplies the latest available gross hires, quits, and layoffs. The [Fed’s September decision](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm) supplies the policy setting.

**Around:** The [BLS household table](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:328), [ADP report](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September), and [St. Louis Fed flow analysis](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september) test different parts of the headline. They should be reconciled by measure and population, not counted as votes for one interpretation.

**After:** As of October 3, the September gross-hiring release and October employment report were still scheduled for November 3 and November 6 respectively. There is no later monthly result to import into the October 2 account. [BLS JOLTS schedule](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_09292026.pdf), [pinned Employment Situation schedule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03.txt).

## Accuracy and Verification

The principal page figures, including +29,000, 4.2 percent, the two revisions totaling −60,000, and 3.0 percent nominal annual hourly-earnings growth, have passage support in the [pinned September release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt). The [PAYEMS levels](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/PAYEMS-2026-10-03.csv) independently verify the page’s arithmetic **within the same BLS data lineage**.

| Additional finding | Exact page bytes | Exact record bytes and correct account |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Minor, grade C:** diffusion-index definition | “the share of 250 private industries adding jobs” | The [BLS footnote](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:440) includes “plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment.” The index is **49.0 under that formula**, not simply the share adding jobs. This is in Just the facts and does not change a seven-answer conclusion. |
| **Verified correction already on the page** | “CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. That figure is mislabeled.” | The [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:305) says “remained at 34.4 hours.” No additional finding. |

**Passage grades, grouped by the answer they can change:** A: BLS headline and July/August revisions, carried in the story. B: survey uncertainty, household balance, Black unemployment, private/government split, historical baseline, gross-hiring distinction, and policy context; these are hidden or absent as detailed above. C: U-6, prime-age participation, longer-term unemployment, wage arithmetic, forecasts, and detailed industry rows; where supplied, Just the facts or Show the work is sufficient. D: release logistics and repetitive coverage that change none of the seven answers. Table A-9’s multiple-jobholder passage would ordinarily be C, but its **incorrect unit** makes the accuracy error Major.

## Source and Corroboration Problems

The page lists 21 records, but record count is not independent confirmation count. The BLS release and its tables are the primary payroll and household records; FRED and ALFRED reproduce BLS series. The page correctly labels PBS as “the same Associated Press dispatch, carried by PBS,” so those two pages are one reporting lineage. CNBC’s preview and follow-up reuse the Dow Jones forecast. The 84,000 and 90,000 forecasts are survey results reported by outlets, not independent observations of employment. [Manifest](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/manifests/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.json), [built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html).

I followed substantive linked records in the pinned coverage, including [ADP](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September), [JOLTS](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_09292026.pdf), [BEA](https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-august-2026), the [Fed statement](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm), and [Glassdoor’s original analysis](https://api.glassdoor.com/blog/glassdoor-employee-confidence-index-september-2026/). They measure different things. No video pin is listed in this event’s manifest, so there were no pinned frames or transcripts to inspect.

## Reconstructed Story

**Established:** On October 2, BLS estimated **29,000 more nonfarm payroll jobs** in September, revised July and August down by a combined **60,000**, and reported **4.2 percent** rounded unemployment. Private payrolls rose **46,000** while government payrolls fell **17,000**. The household survey showed more people employed and participating, alongside more unemployed people; Black unemployment rose from **6.0 to 7.0 percent**. [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:225).

**Strongly supported interpretation:** The payroll side weakened relative to the revised August result and forecasts, but the month does not establish a broad employment contraction or a measured drop in gross hiring. The unrounded unemployment change was small, and a separate private payroll measure was stronger. [BLS technical note](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:706), [St. Louis Fed](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september), [ADP](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September).

**Unknown:** September gross hires, the eventual revisions to September payrolls, September inflation’s relation to wage growth, and the Fed’s October decision. The available records do not establish a single cause for the weak net payroll estimate.

## Remaining Unknowns

The highest-value checks are the next two BLS revisions to September payrolls, November 3 JOLTS data for September hires and separations, the September price releases, and the October 27–28 Fed decision. The March 2026 [preliminary benchmark notice](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/prebmk.htm) also warns that a final benchmark is due later; its −79,000 estimate for **March** is not a September revision. These are future checks, not evidence of an outcome already known.

